<!--Post: 10760--><!--Time: 1110217170-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10760"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Essevi<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Essevi<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 9:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have been going over it in my mind, looking for a reason why Dennis would turn to murder when he had so much going for him. <BR> <BR>I know this is probably an extremely crazy thought, but did he do it all for Paula? <BR> <BR>The papers report that he keeps asking for her, so he must love her. <BR> <BR>In his sick mind, did he murder just to make her feel insecure? So he could protect her because he had experience in law, criminals and security. He could keep her safe from some crazed serial killer. He portrays himself as a tough guy. He was in the service so he had some military training. He installed home security alarms, so he knew how to keep people safe. So, maybe with a killer on the loose, Paula would turn to him for protection. <BR> <BR>Why did he start again? Empty nest, new book soon to be released and mid-life crises. He needed to feel needed by Paula. He wanted those old feelings back when Paula needed him. <BR> <BR>Just a thought as I sit here and wonder why. <BR> <BR>Thoughts?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10760-->
<!--Post: 10761--><!--Time: 1110218031-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10761"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Witchking<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Witchking<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 9:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I watched a two hour long profile Satuday Night on MSNBC on Gary Ridgeway, the Green River Killer. It was apparent this guy was headed for a life of serial murder when he was a teenager who stabbed a 6 year old just to watch him die. He walked away laughing. His home life was a horrid event.  <BR>Dennis seems like he came from far different ciurcumstances. I&#39;ve heard nothing to the contrary but his home life growing up might have been regimented but loving. No alcoholics we know of, no beatings etc. He really breaks the mold here--two parents involved with his life and a healthy relationship with both parents and siblings. <BR> <BR>In this one case I&#39;m inclined to hark back to the movie Midnight Express. There is a character in the asylum where the lead character ends up. One of the fellow inmates at the asylum who seems far more learned than the others albeit no less wacko tells Billy he is a &#39;broken machine&#39; and the asylum is where the broken machines are sent. <BR> <BR>Perhaps, Dennis Rader is indeed a broken machine, broken at a time and place none of us realize, certainly not his family....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10761-->
<!--Post: 10762--><!--Time: 1110218543-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10762"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 10:02 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rader is incapable of love as we understand it. Attachment, yes. His emotional development never progressed beyond a self-centered, infantile stage, which is the hallmark of most psychopathic serial killer&#39;s emotional development.  <BR> <BR>I suspect in the end, it&#39;s ALWAYS about control. He&#39;s attached to Paula, yes. But he&#39;s always controlled her, too.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10762-->
<!--Post: 10766--><!--Time: 1110222760-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10766"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 11:12 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with Steve and Freud&#39;s analysis, of the Id stage never progressing, Bmodule brought up a good point yesterday that may fit into Rader&#39;s thinking pattern, Remember when Rader said, I don&#39; t know when the monster entered into me, etc. I believe that once Rader came home from AF and while working At IGA he realized he wasn&#39;t happy, his mother who may or may not have rented out his room was working at the same place, So Rader goes to work at colemans,  the bosses son I believe the one that was shot, didn&#39;t like rader, and Rader didn&#39;t like being told what to do. Julie was there at Colemans, If Rader remembered Joe from AF or not doesn&#39;t matter, because Rader made a Comment to joe or Julie, I&#39;m gonna Kill that guy. And he does, so the monster is now alive, Bmode can take if from there. Rader is a control freak, He had the tendencies all along, May not have showed up until AF but he was very angry and found a way to stay in control after the Otero&#39;s It became easy, Any one who got in his way would have to pay, If it&#39;s about the rage than the rest of the time he had a normal, so to speak life, Most Serial Killers lose the edge as they get older, Rader personality never really change, He thought it did, because he wasn&#39;t out stalking every night, People around him could see something was wrong, but would not dare question his authority. Wife Children, remained very respectful.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10766-->
<!--Post: 10806--><!--Time: 1110233788-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10806"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 2:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->But Dennis Rader didn&#39;t work at Coleman&#39;s at the same time that Julie Otero worked there. He had left that employment months before she started working there.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10806-->
<!--Post: 10942--><!--Time: 1110312855-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10942"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 08, 2005 - 12:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->sorry about that, Didn&#39;t she work in a jewerly store the same time Rader was wroking for ADT? I will go back and look<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10942-->
<!--Post: 10956--><!--Time: 1110317477-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10956"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lctrip<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lctrip<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 08, 2005 - 1:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Believe that it was Ms. Fox who worked at the jewelry store while Rader was with ADT.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10956-->
<!--Post: 11127--><!--Time: 1110452743-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11127"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 3:05 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Essevi, <BR>You ask a very good question and it&#39;s one that can be raised about many. I mean, why would Bundy kill, when he had the smarts and ambition to make a good career and life for himself?  <BR> <BR>There will always be conflicting theories as to why these creatures murder. Criminologists, shrinks, behavioralists, medical researchers, the man on the street; all will offer similar and different reasons. The best answer for right now would be a combination of what the above people would say. But which portions of what they might say are correct and which are incorrect? It&#39;s all theory and therefore speculative; we look for patterns and can get a decent idea about many things but nobody really &#34;knows,&#34; <i>maybe not even Rader; at least not 100%</i>. <BR> <BR>When I was ten, I shoplifted penny candy -- penny candy? Man, I&#39;m getting old -- from a Ben Franklin <BR>store in Fairfax, VA. The candy added up to maybe forty or fifty cents, yet I had a one dollar bill in my pocket. Was I after a thrill? Or, was I trying to get back at my parents in some way?  Society? Trying to get attention away from my little brother since he was more pampered than I? I really don&#39;t know, and I did the stealing! Got my butt whipped so hard, I bled. Not trying to compare this to Rader&#39;s crimes except to suggest that the answer is not as simple as it may seem. In Rader&#39;s case, a complete answer may be fathomless.  <BR> <BR>In a way, it isn&#39;t completely innacurate to say that there simply is evil in this world and that Rader is one example. Hard as it is for me to admit, he wasn&#39;t completely rotten -- Pax, seriously, thank you for opening my eyes to this -- heck, if nothing else he was good at his city job enforcing codes. Apparently, at church, he was helpful to some. This doesn&#39;t change my harsh punishment beliefs; it merely begs more questions. <BR> <BR>His wife. Is it yet known if he beat her? I ask because Nancy Fox&#39;s father said his daughter was absolutely covered with bruises when he had to confirm her identity. Think of the hell Mr. Fox must have gone through and may still be dealing with. Why did Rader beat up Nancy to such a degree? Did he do the same to the others? To some but not to all? I can&#39;t begin to guess the reason except to agree with some theories and say battery is part of his personality. Was he really an absolute sexual sadist? Or was he more like an anger-retaliatory rapist/murderer? The sadist label is more accurate but doesn&#39;t answer your question.  <BR> <BR>Also regarding Paula. Bundy, Gacy and others were married. They didn&#39;t seem to care what they put their wives through. Bundy, per Ann Rule anyway, had some feeling for his wife and maybe even tried the best he could, at least once in a while, to make her happy. But if we look at his deeds, this is laughable. Still, there&#39;s a chance Rader loved -- to the extent that he was capable of love -- Paula. It doesn&#39;t fit in with popular theories but I won&#39;t dismiss it out-of-hand. Not yet, anyway.  <BR> <BR>Many can sleep more peacefully now, but otherwise, there are no winners here. Eleven families, I include Rader&#39;s -- at least eleven families -- have been destroyed, and a city in Kansas was terrorized and even with the killer&#39;s capture, Wichita will never quite be the same. Nothing brilliant to add and I submit that no one can completely answer what you ask -- which is why it&#39;s a great question. Sorry for a long post.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11127-->
<!--Post: 11128--><!--Time: 1110454305-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11128"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 3:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Witchking, good observation about Rader&#39;s family when he was a kid and growing up. Taken at face value, he doesn&#39;t fit with traditional beliefs but he&#39;s not the first. Dahmer had a pretty good upbringing, as did Cary Stayner. Leopold and Loeb, too. But Rader is an exception, no question.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11128-->
<!--Post: 11133--><!--Time: 1110456506-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11133"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 4:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OOPS! I wrote &#34;eleven families.&#34; It should read &#34;eight&#34; families. Sadly, the toll may still rise. I hope not.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11133-->
<!--Post: 11141--><!--Time: 1110469410-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11141"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 7:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He doesn&#39;t fit the traditional profile for serial killers. I don&#39;t hold much credence to profiling anyway. Profiling is continually being proven to be off the mark. He does have a problem with impulse control-couldn&#39;t control the monster.  We all have had instances when we thought about doing something horrendous, but the difference- we didn&#39;t act on it.  We controlled our impulses.  I think in most psychopaths that they aren&#39;t &#34;wired&#34; to control impulses.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11141-->
<!--Post: 11143--><!--Time: 1110471103-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11143"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 8:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Forgot to add:  <BR> <BR>I think that the impulses were set off by anger and that anger turned to rage. The rage caused him to lash out, and he couldn&#39;t control the impulse &#40;monster&#41; If we were able to find out what caused the anger we might have an answer.  <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t buy the theory that his child hood and developmental factors made him a killer.  Many people have had horrific childhoods and have coped without turning into monsters. I think its nature not nurture! I adhere to the &#34;bad seed&#34; theory. No impulse control! He couldn&#39;t control his impulses but he could control his victims. <BR> <BR>  Just some random thoughts. I am no psychologist, but this seems sensible.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11143-->
<!--Post: 11196--><!--Time: 1110496570-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11196"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 3:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He did it because he could. <BR>His first murder was probably not the Otero family but even if it was he got away with it and found that he liked it. <BR>Pretty soon he needed to kill on a regular basis and it became an addiction and part of him and he continued to get away with.  <BR>The getting away with it aspect became just as important to him as the thrill of the hunt and the kill itself so stopping short of actually turning himself in the only way to highten the getting away with it aspect of the crimes was to taunt the police and the media with clues and a &#39;here I am come and get me attitude&#39;. Let&#39;s play hide and seek and your it. <BR>I certainly am no pychologist but this is how I see it. <BR>I don&#39;t think there ever was a &#39;monster&#39; either. The monster was him and he knew it and he liked it. <BR>His very first killing or near-killing whenever that may have been may have been out of rage or anger or whatever but he found it to be a release and he liked it. He liked the next one better and never really got tired of killing even though he may have stopped it many times.  <BR>It was always there once it began and it will always be there. That part he got right in that the only way to stop it was through death or capture.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11196-->
<!--Post: 11198--><!--Time: 1110497068-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11198"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 3:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Remember when Rader said, &#34;I do not know when the MOnster Entered my body&#34; I agree that it started long before the Otero&#39;s this was the way he had the ultimate control, the book LE found in the shed on serial Killers came after, he was already killing, he wanted an MO that would fit his desires so he started taking ideas from other killers, I honestly feel he started killing a long time ago, In times of emotional stress or anger<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11198-->
<!--Post: 11205--><!--Time: 1110500096-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11205"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 4:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Things that I&#39;ve read about Rader&#39;s youth were all indicative of a normal happy childhood. Even in his class reunion questionnaire, he made it sound like he had things really good, &#40;i.e., didn&#39;t have a favorite teacher, loved them all, etc.&#41; But in other things I&#39;ve read, it seems like he was never one to laugh, or take things lightly. I wonder about his adolescent personal life. There hasn&#39;t been a string of ex-girlfriends that came forward to talk about what it was like dating him, &#40;not that I know of anyways&#41; which is something that normally happens in cases like these. This is my theory, and take it lightly, because I was so wrong in my personal profile of who BTK was... LOL <BR> <BR>I picture young DTDC as a serious student. He did his work, and went home. If there were things that he did that were extracurricular, it was church related, or possibly something for special needs &#40;dyslexia&#41;. Being this way doesn&#39;t get a kid in the popular category. I bet the cool kids teased him, &#40;you know, the timeless &#34;pecking order&#34;&#41; and this may have sparked the bureaucratic bully that the people of Park City have come to know. Once he started getting into girls, they seemed out of his league. I think he may possibly have asked a certain girl out, but she rejected him, and shattered his self-esteem. So, the fantasies started. He probably dreamed of this girl, and since he couldn&#39;t have her, this introduced the bondage fantasies. Eventually he met Paula, who didn&#39;t reject him, deemed her perfect, formed a bond with her, and made her his wife. I figure that Dennis really was into his S&amp;M fantasies, but I think due to the relious card, he probably couldn&#39;t bring himself to ask Paula to act any of it out. I bet he did solicit prostitutes, &#40;I wouldn&#39;t be surprised if his first victim was a call girl.&#41; But it wasn&#39;t enough for him. I bet he found the experience with them too staged, and I bet he found the girl&#40;s&#41; to be too dirty. He needed it to be a victim. He needed the fear, he needed to control, he needed to torture, but most of all, he needed it to all be real. He had the urge to act out his fantasy as he pictured it, and that couldn&#39;t stay suppressed, and this urge is what I feel as if he deemed &#34;the monster.&#34; A better person could have these feelings and not need to act on them, but to this waste of functioning organs.....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11205-->
<!--Post: 11250--><!--Time: 1110528085-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11250"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 12:01 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It has never set very well with me that the Otero&#39;s were the first. Not unless you accept the idea that he had a reason to do them. I think all his activities between the time he got out of high school and Aug. 1966 are in play right now.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11250-->
<!--Post: 11259--><!--Time: 1110538116-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11259"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 2:48 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Linnieskid, I agree with you to a large degree about profiling, especially psychological profiling, but not about impulse control. These crimes were planned extremely well; the commission of them wasn&#39;t perfect, Otero and Bright, esp. Bright, were almost reckless. This may indicate lack of experience or perhaps the cliched &#34;rage&#34; that many murderers experience as they begin to kill. Nancy Fox being so bruised, which to me hints at most of the others also being recipients of battery does look like rage to me, and anyone in a rage is not in complete control of a situation. And I still say his driving of the Otero car to a lot was not in keeping with &#34;organized&#34; behavior. Meaning, and don&#39;t take this the wrong way, but a white male in the Otero&#39;s car would stick out; the Oteros hadn&#39;t &#34;just moved in.&#34; They&#39;d been in residence for several months and at least some neighbors might have noticed their car being driven by a stranger. <BR> <BR>I do believe Otero was his first attack in the Wichita area. Earlier unsolved crimes in other locales which Rader inhabited should certainly be looked at as much as can be realistically expected, I agree!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11259-->
<!--Post: 11260--><!--Time: 1110539075-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11260"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 3:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Linnieskid, you wrote, <BR> <BR>&#34;Profiling is continually being proven to be off the mark.&#34; <BR> <BR>Not always, but frequently. Reminds me. The 1980s &#34;Ghostbusters&#34; task-force. This force included profilers &#40;read &#34;Quantico&#34;&#41; and input from same. Not one soul on the Ghostbusters task-force thought Vicki Wegerle was a BTK victim. Not one. Unless A&amp;E presented misleading information about this specific example.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11260-->
<!--Post: 11302--><!--Time: 1110567201-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11302"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 10:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George:  Look at Bundy, for the most part-organized.  He himself stated &#40;loose translation&#41; that the urge &#40;impulse&#41;would hit, and build for several weeks, days etc.  The urge never went away.  Dahmer mentioned the same thing in his last interview.  To the point:  they were able to control the urge for limited periods of time.  But inevitably the impulse reached a crecsedno and they &#34;went for it&#34;. Translates to delayed action on impulse, but none the less the impulse took over and was acted on.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11302-->
<!--Post: 11316--><!--Time: 1110583902-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11316"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 3:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George, what are your thoughts on geographical profiling? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11316-->
